Feature / Capability	New Zealand Catalogues	USGS / ComCat	Swiss SED / ECOS / EPOS	Comments & Gaps
Core event parameters	NZ catalogues (GeoNet, integrated catalog efforts) include origin time, latitude/longitude, depth, magnitude, and in many cases metadata like phase picks, station counts, gaps, etc.	ComCat includes hypocenter, magnitudes, phase picks and amplitudes from contributing networks, along with other products (moment tensors, macroseismic data) (USGS)	SED’s ECOS catalog includes historical + instrumental events, uniform Mw conversions, uncertainties, macroseismic intensities; integrates with EPOS standards (ResearchGate)	NZ matches many core capabilities but sometimes lacks full uncertainty propagation or detailed metadata for all events.
Metadata & documentation / processing provenance	Some catalogues document processing pipelines, velocity model references, and quality metrics; but in many cases, metadata and methods are incomplete or inconsistent across catalogs.	USGS maintains metadata standards and data dictionaries, requiring documentation of methods, accuracy/precision, field definitions (USGS)	SED (Switzerland) adheres to international standards (FDSN, StationXML, QuakeML) and robust metadata / dissemination practices (NHESS)	NZ should aim for consistent, centralised metadata policies across all catalogues.
Versioning, updates, and review	GeoNet catalog supports rapid + review workflows. Some catalogues are relocated or reprocessed (e.g. research relocations).	ComCat supports versioned updates, multiple origins per event, and revisions over time.	SED / EPOS structure supports unified access to evolving datasets, harmonization across Europe.	Full version control (DOIs, changelogs) is an area where NZ can formalize practice more consistently.
Access & searchability / APIs	GeoNet offers web search (Quake Search), FDSN event service (QuakeML), CSV/GeoJSON export.	USGS supports FDSN event API, feeds (GeoJSON, CSV), search interfaces (USGS Earthquakes)	EPOS provides integrated access to seismic products (waveform, parametric data) via standardized services (Epos EU)	NZ is well-aligned technically; enhancements could focus on federated discovery, cross-catalog querying.
Historical / pre-instrumental integration	NZ includes some pre-instrumental events (via historical catalogs) but with greater uncertainty and lower detail.	USGS / ISC / historical datasets often include large historic events, though with variable completeness.	SED’s ECOS compiles historical catalogs back many centuries, including macroseismic fields and uncertainty estimates (ResearchGate)	NZ’s historical catalogs need stronger integration, improved metadata, and uncertainty modeling.
Uncertainty and quality metrics	Many modern NZ catalogues provide uncertainty estimates (horizontal/vertical errors, magnitude error) but not universally.	ComCat includes quality metrics (e.g. RMS, number of stations, azimuthal gap) (USGS Earthquakes)	SED strongly emphasizes uncertainty and solution quality, linked to EPOS standards (NHESS)	Full uncertainty reporting in every catalog entry is still a gap to be filled across NZ datasets.
Multi-catalog / federated linking	NZ catalogs are mostly produced separately; limited linking or mapping across catalogues.	ComCat aggregates from many networks, linking multiple origins under one event.	EPOS aims for harmonized, interoperable catalogs across Europe; SED participates in cross-border data exchange. (Epos EU)	NZ could build an “Event ID mapping service” and federated portal to link catalogues.
Special-purpose catalogs (mechanisms, slip, etc.)	NZ has research catalogs for focal mechanisms, moment tensors, slow slip, but often as standalone datasets with limited integration.	USGS/ComCat hosts moment tensor solutions and additional products tied to events.	EPOS integrates fault, slip, seismological products with seismic catalogs.	NZ can better integrate special-purpose catalogs into the main framework.
Compliance with standards (QuakeML, FDSN, etc.)	GeoNet supports FDSN/QuakeML, though not all catalogues may fully adhere.	USGS supports FDSN and QuakeML formats in APIs and exports.	SED / EPOS adhere to QuakeML, StationXML, FDSN, metadata standards. (NHESS)	NZ is on the right track; ensuring full compliance across catalogs is a key goal.
Sustainability / governance / community	Catalog maintenance is mostly under GNS / GeoNet or individual researchers; less formal cross-catalog governance exists.	USGS / ANSS / NEIC have formal governance, funding, and institutional roles.	EPOS / SED have robust institutional backing and cross-national coordination.	NZ would benefit from a formal governance structure, roles, and funding for federated cataloging.

Summary of Workshop Outcomes on Earthquake Catalogues in Aotearoa New Zealand
Catalogue Types and Examples
Participants identified a broad spectrum of catalogue types across New Zealand:
Volcano-specific microseismic catalogues (Taupō, Whakaari, Ruapehu, geothermal areas)


Historical compilations (pre-1940 events, oral/written records, NSHM inputs)


National-scale catalogues (GeoNet rapid/review, GeoNet CMT, strong motion datasets)


Local/regional deployments (Alpine Fault OBS, Hikurangi experiments, Kaikōura/Cook Strait aftershock sequences, TVZ experiments)


Moment tensor, focal mechanism, finite-fault and stress-drop catalogues (Ristau, GCMT, IRIS, research-specific inversions)


Slow slip, paleoseismic, and geodetic-derived catalogues (e.g., Litchfield palaeoseismic, offshore geodetic detections)


Temporary/field deployments (SAMBA, COSA, HOBITSS, PTANGO, etc.)


Rapid response/emergency catalogues (GeoNet, response teams, template-matching)


Research-focused or method-specific catalogues (machine learning, relocations, waveform inversions, strong motion derived)


International combined catalogues (ISC, COMCAT, EMSC).


Common Strengths
Local studies: completeness, precise depths, uniform methodology within regions.


National products: consistency, continuity, accessibility, manual checks.


Temporary deployments: high-quality velocity models, good event location/depth precision.


Moment tensor/source catalogues: provide critical kinematic and stress information for hazard and tectonics.


Research relocations: improved depths and completeness, well documented methods.


Rapid response catalogues: fast availability, crucial for emergency and tsunami response.


Common Weaknesses
Fragmentation and ad hoc coverage: many catalogues exist but are poorly documented or hard to find.


Variable completeness: in time and space, especially for small events or during evolving network deployments.


Inconsistent processing: differences in magnitude scales, velocity models, and methods limit comparability.


Metadata gaps: missing pick uncertainties, processing history, or clear description of methods.


Limited access: especially for legacy data, temporary deployments, or research-specific catalogues.


Rapid response limitations: automated catalogues often incomplete or inaccurate prior to expert review.


Historical data: large uncertainties, poor depth/location quality, lack of small events.


Geodetic/paleoseismic: sparse coverage, limited precision in timing.


Value to Unlock
Key opportunities for improvement include:
Centralised data hub: a federated portal that hosts or links all NZ catalogues.


Standardised formats and lexicon: CSV, QuakeML, ObsPy-compatible; consistent metadata and naming.


Version control and DOIs: citable releases, clear provenance, explicit uncertainties.


Improved metadata: include station information, velocity models, methodology, uncertainties.


Legacy data integration: digitisation, reconciliation, and metadata improvement.


National community velocity model: multi-resolution (1D and 3D) model agreed upon.


Regular updates: a “catalogue of catalogues” revised ~every 5 years.


Openness and accessibility: open APIs, FAIR compliance, minimal barriers to access.


Community governance: defined roles, responsibilities, and sustainability planning.



Next Steps
Develop a preliminary framework: minimum metadata, standard formats, clear governance.


Pilot projects: prototype portal, ingestion of legacy datasets, integration of temporary deployments.


Define roles and responsibilities: who curates, reviews, and maintains catalogue releases.


Establish metrics of FAIRness: findability, accessibility, interoperability, reusability.


Promote interoperability with global systems (USGS ComCat, ISC, EMSC, EPOS).


Support modern methodologies (template matching, ML, DAS, dense arrays) with transparency and uncertainty reporting.


Developing a Framework for Creating Earthquake Catalogue 
Introduction 
Earthquake catalog are essential for understanding seismic activity, assessing hazards, and improving earthquake forecasting models. A comprehensive and standardised framework for creating and maintaining earthquake catalogs is crucial to ensure consistency, accuracy, and usability. This paper outlines key principles, components, and recommendations for developing such a framework.
Objectives of the Framework
To standardise the collection and classification of earthquake data.
To ensure compatibility and interoperability between catalogs.
…
Core Components of the Framework
Data Standards and Format
Metadata Requirements: Include event ID, location, depth, magnitude, time, and source information.
Standardised Formats: Use established formats such as QuakeML or CSV to facilitate data sharing.
 Data Quality Control
…


Tools and Technologies
Automation Tools: Implement automated pipelines for data ingestion, processing, and updates
Database Management Systems: Use scalable databases (e.g., PostgreSQL) to store and manage large datasets.


Interoperability and Integration
Global Compatibility: Ensure the catalog can integrate with other international catalogs (e.g., ISC, USGS).
Data Sharing Protocols:
….
Use Cases and Applications
Seismic Hazard Assessment: Supporting national and regional hazard models.
Earthquake Forecasting: Providing input data for statistical and machine learning models.
…..
Recommendations
Adopt Common Standards: Use globally recognised standards for data collection and reporting.
Collaborate Across Organisations: Foster partnerships to ensure comprehensive data coverage.
Technology: Develop and maintain infrastructure to support scalable and reliable catalogs.
Regular Updates and Reviews: Periodically review and improve the framework based on new research and technological advancements.
…
Conclusion
Creating a standardised framework for earthquake catalogs is critical for advancing seismic research and improving public safety. By adopting best practices in data collection, quality control, and integration, researchers and stakeholders can ensure that earthquake data remains a reliable foundation for scientific research.

Suggest that the motivation for this collection is to do a lit review of NZ catalogs to (1) compare and contrast; (2) come up with common set of minimum attributes for future catalog creation (benchmarked against what is being done overseas, e.g., Switz was mentioned); and (3) GAP analysis of what is missing and potential near-term community efforts to achieve a world-renowned federated/harmonised catalog for the purpose of use in earthquake science and engineering. 
